### Assert Not Contains Only

Source: https://docs.phpunit.de/en/13.0/assertions.html

The inverse of assertContainsOnly(). Reports an error if the haystack *does* contain only elements of the specified type. Note: This method is deprecated as of PHPUnit 12.0 and will be removed in PHPUnit 13.

```PHP
<?php declare(strict_types=1);
use PHPUnit\Framework\TestCase;

final class NotContainsOnlyTest extends TestCase
{
    public function testFailure(): void
    {
        $this->assertNotContainsOnly('string', ['1', 2, '3']);
    }
}

```

--------------------------------

### Assert Contains Only (Native Type)

Source: https://docs.phpunit.de/en/13.0/assertions.html

Reports an error if the haystack does not contain only elements of the specified native PHP type. Note: This method is deprecated as of PHPUnit 12.0 and will be removed in PHPUnit 13.

```PHP
<?php declare(strict_types=1);
use PHPUnit\Framework\TestCase;

final class ContainsOnlyNativeTypeTest extends TestCase
{
    public function testFailure(): void
    {
        $this->assertContainsOnly('integer', ['1', '2', 3], true);
    }
}

```

### Organizing Tests > The XML Configuration File > The `<phpunit>` Element

Source: https://docs.phpunit.de/en/13.0/organizing-tests.html

The XML configuration file for PHPUnit offers extensive customization through various elements and attributes. The root `<phpunit>` element controls global settings such as backup behavior, bootstrap scripts, cache directories, output formatting (colors, columns), garbage collection, and error/defect handling (stopOnDefect, stopOnError, failOnWarning, etc.). It also manages test execution order, dependency resolution, and detailed output display.

--------------------------------

### PHPUnit CLI Options > The XML Configuration File > The <phpunit> Element

Source: https://docs.phpunit.de/en/13.0/cli-options.html

The `<phpunit>` element in the XML configuration file offers extensive control over test execution and reporting. Attributes like `beStrictAboutChangesToGlobalState`, `beStrictAboutOutputDuringTests`, and `beStrictAboutTestsThatDoNotTestAnything` enforce stricter testing practices. Time-related attributes such as `enforceTimeLimit`, `defaultTimeLimit`, `timeoutForSmallTests`, `timeoutForMediumTests`, and `timeoutForLargeTests` allow for fine-grained control over test execution duration. Other attributes manage test suite defaults (`defaultTestSuite`), output streams (`stderr`), and dependency resolution (`resolveDependencies`).

--------------------------------

### Composing a Test Suite Using XML Configuration

Source: https://docs.phpunit.de/en/13.0/organizing-tests.html

When using an XML configuration file for PHPUnit, it is important to reference the correct schema definition that corresponds to the PHPUnit version being used. For example, the schema for PHPUnit 13.0 can be found at https://schema.phpunit.de/13.0/phpunit.xsd.

